<Base title="Chemistry Library — FANNYMAGNET" description="Evidence-led explanations of the chemistry behind sports performance and recovery."><main class="page"><a class="back" href="/">← FANNYMAGNET</a><p class="kicker">CHEMISTRY / FIELD LIBRARY</p><h1>Under the surface.</h1><p class="intro">A visual index of the molecules, systems and claims that shape performance.</p><div class="topics"><article id="protein"><span>01 / MACRONUTRIENT</span><h2>Protein & amino acids</h2><p>What it is: the raw material for tissue repair and adaptation. Research supports adequate total protein, while timing and product differences remain more nuanced than marketing suggests.</p></article><article id="creatine"><span>02 / SMALL MOLECULE</span><h2>Creatine monohydrate</h2><p>What it is: a compound involved in rapid energy recycling. It is among the most studied sports supplements; dose, individual response and product quality still matter.</p></article><article><span>03 / ENERGY SYSTEM</span><h2>Carbohydrates & electrolytes</h2><p>Fuel and fluid balance influence training output. Needs depend on session length, sweat loss, climate and the person doing the work.</p></article><article><span>04 / STIMULANTS</span><h2>Caffeine</h2><p>Research suggests caffeine can affect alertness and perceived effort. Sensitivity, sleep and total intake are central safety considerations.</p></article></div><a class="cta" href="/store">SHOP VERIFIED BASICS ↗</a></main></Base>
